I've played around with it a bit. Some brief feedback:
 
Engineers love trends and graphs. Make it much, much easier to see a nice trend of whatever data for all ingested batches. I might even make it the landing page for the workspace.
Same for your signal alerts of deviations - show the evidence visually so I can make a call.
 
I'm not super clear what the 'simulation' section is really meant for. Perhaps it's just under development. You may want to provide example workflows for that one.
 
The ingestion features look nice and useful.  Previews of the documents being ingested might be nice, to orient the user to what's what - filenames alone can be confusing. Especially for PDF dumps. 
 
When ingesting data and showing errors in the 'To Check' section, showing more context of the specific doc and area would be nice. Often I want to see the surrounding data to know if a given thing is accurate or not. Try to make sure I never have to open the original document myself to do what I need to do - I'm much more likely to use your software if it's all internal and there's no switching friction. 
 
I unfortunately don't have any public data I can use right now, but I'd like to. I'll let you know if I find a good dataset so I can experiment more. 
 
Finally, this is a personal/'taste' thing - take it or leave it, up to you. I hate chat assistant based workflows, especially for data management. I should not have to type out the fields I want in an analysis, I should be selecting them from a dropdown menu. Your assistant functionality seems good but I'd much prefer if it was more of a 'superpowered search and tool fetcher' than actually running analyses in the chat-model window. Additionally, chat assistants are slow by their very nature. I assume you're wrapping someone else's inference provider so I know there's not a ton you can do about that but a basic request took me 230 seconds, which is about 20x longer than my focused attention span can manage waiting for results. It's difficult to maintain my own context on the data and the decisions I'd be making if it's running at this speed.
 
I would caution you on a few things - I'm not sure how big your TAM is and I am sure that market penetration will be quite difficult. You may run into competitors as well - especially dangerous ones would be equipment manufacturers and CRO/CMOs who make their own dashboards and will always have better control over integration than you will as a third-party. 
 
Overall, a cool tool. Good work so far and I appreciate you reaching out.